$DIR/main.p:17:16: cannot use Square{…} (value of struct type Square) as Shape value in variable declaration: Square does not implement Shape (method Area has pointer receiver)
    s is declared as Shape, so it can only hold values whose type has every
    method of Shape.
    Shape needs a method Area() int (declared on line 9). Square has it, but the
    method is declared on *Square, so only a pointer to a Square has it, not a
    Square value. A type satisfies an interface only if its own method set has
    every method.
    Use a pointer:
        &Square{…}
    (see "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9)
    (run "pluto explain E0021" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:23:9: cannot use Square{…} (value of struct type Square) as Shape value in return statement: Square does not implement Shape (method Area has pointer receiver)
    build is declared on line 22 to return Shape, so it can only hold values
    whose type has every method of Shape.
    Shape needs a method Area() int (declared on line 9). Square has it, but the
    method is declared on *Square, so only a pointer to a Square has it, not a
    Square value. A type satisfies an interface only if its own method set has
    every method. A pointer to a value made here would point into the frame of
    the function, which is reclaimed when it returns (language spec section
    18.3).
    Build the value on the heap with new, which the caller owns:
        new(Square{...})
    The interface takes ownership of what it holds only if it is declared owner
    (language spec section 7.9):
        type Shape owner interface {
            ...
        }
    (see "Interface types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.9)
    (run "pluto explain E0021" for a longer explanation)

